<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Messali Hadj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Messali Hadj (1898–1974) was a founder of Algerian nationalism who led the North African Star, then the Algerian People's Party and the Movement for the Triumph of Democratic Liberties. He was the first major figure to demand full independence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. When younger activists founded the FLN in 1954, he set up the rival Algerian National Movement, and the two waged a bloody struggle during the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/messali-hadj.jpg" alt="Messali Hadj" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maccc · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messali_Hadj_Messali_Clean.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmed Ben Messali Hadj was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, the son of Hadj Ahmed Messali, who was of Turkish origin, and of Ftéma Sari Ali Hadj-Eddine, daughter of a qadi of the Darqawiyya brotherhood. He went to a local French primary school and received a religious education shaped by the Darqawiyya Sufi order. He served in the French army from 1918 to 1921, becoming a sergeant in 1919, and in October 1923 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he sold Tlemceni handicrafts, took Arabic courses and met his wife, Émilie Busquant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Abdelkader Hadj Ali recruited him to the colonial commission of the French Communist Party in 1925. Messali founded the Étoile Nord-Africaine in 1926 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1927 set out its demands: the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigenat">Indigénat</a>, trade union rights, education for all and social welfare. The party was banned in 1929, and Messali was often jailed or exiled. In exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> he met Shakib Arslan and turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a>, founding the Parti du Peuple Algérien in March 1937. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> court sentenced him in March 1941 to 16 years of hard labour.</p><p>Amnestied after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, he was deported again to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> in 1945, which helped set off the riots at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setif">Sétif</a> in May 1945, after which the army and police killed approximately 10,000 Muslims. In 1946 he founded the Mouvement pour le triomphe des libertés démocratiques, and in 1947 the party approved the creation of the Organisation spéciale. His insistence on Arabism alienated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabyles">Kabyles</a> and contributed to the Berberist crisis of 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The war of independence began in 1954 against his wishes. The FLN's armed wing destroyed his movement's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> early in the war, and the fighting moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the so-called café wars; the author Remy Mauduit puts the dead at 10,000. Messali supported the proposals of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> in 1958. After independence in 1962 he failed to turn his movement into a legal party. He died in exile in France and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>. He is often called the father of Algerian nationalism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Messali Hadj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Colonial rule also produced the nationalism that ended it: the reformist Association of Algerian Muslim Ulama founded by Abdelhamid Ben Badis in 1931, the independence movement of <b>Messali Hadj</b> and the liberal nationalism of Ferhat Abbas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Gouvieux, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.88,-1.32&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Tlemcen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4517 901 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Messali Hadj"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4967 1171h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4967 1171h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.88,-1.32&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
